sections.content:
The pattern, verified conceptually:

```js
// WITHOUT delegation — one listener per button, re-bind on every add
list.querySelectorAll('button').forEach(b => b.addEventListener('click', onClick));
// a new <li><button/></li> added later? its button has NO listener. Re-bind everything.

// WITH delegation — one listener, current AND future children covered
list.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  // e.target = the ACTUAL element clicked (deepest)
  // this  = the element the listener is ON (the list)
  if (e.target.matches('button.delete')) {
    const li = e.target.closest('li');   // walk up to the row
    deleteItem(li.dataset.id);
  }
});
// dynamically added rows just work — the listener is on the stable parent.
```

Why it works (the mechanics to name): events bubble — after the target's own handlers run, the event travels up (target → ... → document → window), firing capture-outward listeners on each ancestor. A parent listener therefore sees every descendant's clicks. e.target stays the original target throughout; e.currentTarget is whatever element the handler is attached to.

The three wins (the interview answer):
- Performance at scale: 10,000 rows = 10,000 listeners without delegation, 1 with. Listener count costs memory and bind time.
- Dynamic content: children added/removed after binding need no re-binding — the parent never changed. This is THE pattern for lists, tables, chat feeds.
- Simpler lifecycle: attach once, remove once — no bookkeeping per child.

The boundary (when delegation does NOT fit): mousemove/mouseover fire constantly and need per-element handling; e.target checking gets brittle when children of the intended target are also clickable (you need closest() to normalize); and non-bubbling events (focus/blur — use focusin/focusout, which bubble) don't delegate naively.
